BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//Monash Events//EN
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:CHE Seminar Series: The abuse trap
DTSTART:20260916T120000
DTEND:20260916T130000
DESCRIPTION:This paper is motivated by the fact that domestic abuse recurs
  within couples\, but that nevertheless only a small share of  women lea
 ve abusive partnerships. We investigate women's intention to leave conditi
 onal on abuse as a function of their beliefs over the risk of future abuse
 . Possibly contrary to expectation\, we find that women who anticipate abu
 se state that they are less likely to leave. This association is stronger 
 for psychological than for physical abuse\, and stronger among women who h
 ave previously suffered abuse. It is similar across the distributions of e
 ducation\, employment and income\, particularly if the woman has already b
 een abused. We find limited support for competing explanations\, including
  selection into abusive partnerships\, sabotage of outside options\, gener
 alized pessimism and depression. We then look at actual rates of leaving c
 onditional upon abuse\, and find that these are a lot lower than intended 
 rates. We argue that our findings are consistent with abusive men acting t
 o make it costly for their partners to leave\, for instance by instilling 
 fear. It is also consistent with our finding that women tend to under-esti
 mate abuse risk\, and most so when they are at higher risk\, which charact
 erises women who are depressed\, and women who have witnessed parental abu
 se as children.\n\nSpeaker profile\n\n<a href="https://sites.google.com/si
 te/aldelaresearch/" target="_blank">Adeline Delavande</a> is Professor of 
 Economics at the University of Technology Sydney and Nova School of Busine
 ss and Economics. Her research examines how individuals form beliefs and m
 ake decisions under uncertainty\, with applications in economic demography
 \, health\, education\, and development economics. A central feature of he
 r work is the collection and analysis of probabilistic expectations data t
 o better understand individual behaviour and evaluate policy interventions
 . Her research frequently combines original survey design\, field experime
 nts\, and applied microeconometric methods\, often through interdisciplina
 ry collaborations with researchers and practitioners in health and other f
 ields.\n\nWeekly seminar series\n\nAs part of our Centre's vibrant researc
 h culture\, we host a weekly seminar series. Visiting and invited research
 ers present current research relating to the economics of health and wellb
 eing\, and the healthcare sector. Visitors are welcome to join these sessi
 ons where discussion and debate is encouraged.\n\nFor further information 
 on our seminar series\, please contact <a href="mailto:Trong-Anh.Trinh@mo
 nash.edu">Trong-Anh.Trinh@monash.edu</a>\n\n<a class="btn" href="https://m
 onash.zoom.us/j/83600802355?pwd=1F4DXlfQDO7MHpjzEWsMC6dZbAX5do.1 ">Join Zo
 om</a>
LOCATION:Caulfield campus\, Building H\, level 9\, room H9.21
URL:https://www.monash.edu/business/events/che-seminar-series-the-abuse-tr
 ap
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:PLIDA Data Training Workshop 2026
DTSTART:20261015T090000
DTEND:20261016T170000
DESCRIPTION:<strong>PLIDA in Practice - Using linked administrative data f
 or health\, disability and social policy research</strong>\n\nThe Person L
 evel Integrated Data Asset (<a href="https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/dat
 a-integration/integrated-data/person-level-integrated-data-asset-plida" ta
 rget="_blank">PLIDA</a>) is one of Australia’s most important research a
 ssets. It offers major opportunities for health\, disability\, social poli
 cy and economics research\, but using it well requires a clear understandi
 ng of how the data is structured\, what the records measure\, how access a
 nd output rules work\, and how research questions can be translated into f
 easible DataLab projects.\n\nHosted by Monash Business School’s <a href=
 "https://www.monash.edu/business/che">Centre for Health Economics</a>\, th
 is short course is offered as two linked one-day workshops. Participants m
 ay register for either workshop or for both linked workshops. The two work
 shops are designed to be complementary\, with Day 1 providing a structured
  understanding of PLIDA\, and Day 2 focusing on more applied aspects of wo
 rking with the asset.\n\nThe course is designed for analysts\, researchers
  and policy professionals in government\, academia\, industry and the not-
 for-profit sector who use\, manage\, commission or are considering PLIDA-b
 ased research and evaluation projects.\n\nThe course will not involve live
  access to PLIDA data. Instead\, it will use approved examples\, cleared o
 utputs and practical workflow guidance to build participants’ understand
 ing of how PLIDA can be used in practice.\n\nRegistrations\n\n<a class="bt
 n" href="https://events.humanitix.com/2026-plida-data-training-workshop">R
 egister here</a>\n\nData access\n\nNo existing PLIDA or DataLab access is 
 required. The course will not involve live analysis of PLIDA data.\n\nSoft
 ware\n\nThe course is not a hands-on coding workshop. Examples may refer t
 o analytical workflows\, but participants are not required to use a specif
 ic statistical package.\n\nFacilitators\n\n<a href="https://research.monas
 h.edu/en/persons/david-johnston/" target="_blank">Prof David Johnston</a>
 \, Centre for Health Economics\, Monash Business School\n\n<a href="https:
 //research.monash.edu/en/persons/nicole-black/" target="_blank">A/Prof Nic
 ole Black</a>\, Centre for Health Economics\, Monash Business School\n\n<a
  href="https://research.monash.edu/en/persons/anh-trinh/" target="_blank">
 Dr Anh Trinh</a>\, Centre for Health Economics\, Monash Business School\n
 \n<a href="https://research.monash.edu/en/persons/francis-graham/" target=
 "_blank">Dr Francis Graham</a>\, Centre for Health Economics\, Monash Busi
 ness School\n\nWhat will you learn?\n\nBy the end of the course\, particip
 ants will have a stronger understanding of:\n\nWho should attend?\n\nThis 
 course is designed for people working in or alongside health\, disability 
 and social policymaking\, who want to use linked administrative data more 
 effectively. It will be particularly relevant for:\n\nThe course is suitab
 le for people who are new to PLIDA\, as well as those who have started wor
 king with the asset and want a clearer understanding of its structure\, in
 terpretation and practical use.\n\n<!-- false -->\n<div class="accordion n
 ested-accordion js-frontend-complex-accordion" id="Component-4420527" role
 ="tablist">\n<div class="hidden">\n<span class="hidden nested-accordion__e
 xpand-text">View</span>\n<span class="hidden nested-accordion__collapse_te
 xt">Close</span>\n</div>\n<section class="panel section1 Prerequisites-1 c
 lose">\n<h3 aria-controls="Prerequisites-1" aria-expanded="false" class="a
 ccTitle" data-click-event="monash.accordion.toggle" data-expanded-by-defau
 lt="false" data-position="1" data-title="Prerequisites" data-type="nested"
  id="Prerequisites-1" role="tab">\n<span class="accTitle__text">Prerequisi
 tes</span>\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-wrapper">\n<a class="accTitle__t
 oggle" href="#Prerequisites-1" rel="1">\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-tex
 t">View</span>\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-arrow"><img alt="View" src="
 https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/file/0011/2179901/arrow-outline-right
 .svg"/></span>\n</a>\n</span>\n</h3>\n<div aria-labelledby="Prerequisites-
 1" class="accContent" role="tabpanel" style="display: none\;">\n<div class
 ="accContent__inner">\n<div class="accContent__wrapper">\n<div id="compone
 nt_4420503">\n<p>Participants should have some familiarity with quantitati
 ve research\, policy analysis or applied data analysis. Prior experience w
 ith PLIDA is not required.</p><p>Day 2’s workshop will be most useful fo
 r participants who are considering\, developing\, managing or already work
 ing on PLIDA-based projects. Coding experience is useful but not required.
 </p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</section>\n<section class="panel se
 ction2 Course_structure-2">\n<h3 aria-controls="Course_structure-2" aria-e
 xpanded="false" class="accTitle" data-click-event="monash.accordion.toggle
 " data-position="2" data-title="Course structure" data-type="nested" id="C
 ourse_structure-2" role="tab">\n<span class="accTitle__text">Course struct
 ure</span>\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-wrapper">\n<a class="accTitle__t
 oggle" href="#Course_structure-2" rel="2">\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-
 text">View</span>\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-arrow"><img alt="View" sr
 c="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/file/0011/2179901/arrow-outline-ri
 ght.svg"/></span>\n</a>\n</span>\n</h3>\n<div aria-labelledby="Course_stru
 cture-2" class="accContent" role="tabpanel" style="display: none\;">\n<div
  class="accContent__inner">\n<div class="accContent__wrapper">\n<div class
 ="graphite-accordion-component">\n<!-- false -->\n<section aria-label="acc
 ordion-4420538">\n<div class="graphite-accordion" id="Component-4420538" s
 tyle="">\n<ul class="graphite-accordion__list">\n<li class="graphite-accor
 dion__item section1 Day_1_Understanding_PLIDA-1">\n<div style="scroll-marg
 in-top: 50px\;">\n<button aria-controls="Day_1_Understanding_PLIDA-1-conte
 nt" aria-expanded="false" class="graphite-accordion__button" data-expanded
 -by-default="false" id="Day_1_Understanding_PLIDA-1">\n                   
                              Day 1: Understanding PLIDA\n                 
                            </button>\n</div>\n<div aria-labelledby="Day_1_
 Understanding_PLIDA-1" class="graphite-accordion__content" data-landmark-i
 ndex="1" id="Day_1_Understanding_PLIDA-1-content" role="region" style="dis
 play: none\;">\n<h4>What is PLIDA?</h4><p>This session provides a factual 
 overview of PLIDA as a national linked administrative data asset. It will 
 cover the population base\, broad data domains\, modular structure\, longi
 tudinal coverage\, major source datasets and the general logic of linkage.
 </p><h4>Administrative data and measurement</h4><p>This session examines t
 he interpretation of administrative records for research and policy analys
 is. It will focus on what administrative data can and cannot measure\, inc
 luding distinctions between service use and need\, eligibility and receipt
 \, diagnosis and administrative classification\, treatment and payment rec
 ords\, and socioeconomic circumstances and administrative proxies.</p><h4>
 PLIDA for health and disability policy research</h4><p>This session uses r
 eal PLIDA outputs and applied examples to show what PLIDA can contribute t
 o health\, disability and social policy research. The session will focus o
 n how the data have been used in practice\, what kinds of questions they c
 an answer well\, and where their limitations become important.</p><h4>Acce
 ss governance and outputs</h4><p>This session explains the practical requi
 rements for undertaking a PLIDA project. It will cover project application
 s\, module requests\, data custodian approvals\, DataLab access\, costs\, 
 timelines\, researcher obligations\, safe use rules and output clearance r
 equirements.</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n<li class="graphite-accordion__item secti
 on2 Day_2_Working_with_PLIDA-2">\n<div style="scroll-margin-top: 50px\;">
 \n<button aria-controls="Day_2_Working_with_PLIDA-2-content" aria-expanded
 ="false" class="graphite-accordion__button" id="Day_2_Working_with_PLIDA-2
 ">\n                                                Day 2: Working with PL
 IDA\n                                            </button>\n</div>\n<div a
 ria-labelledby="Day_2_Working_with_PLIDA-2" class="graphite-accordion__con
 tent" data-landmark-index="2" id="Day_2_Working_with_PLIDA-2-content" role
 ="region" style="display: none\;">\n<h4>PLIDA data architecture</h4><p>Thi
 s session provides a more technical overview of how PLIDA is structured fo
 r analysis. It will cover the spine\, linkage keys\, module-specific ident
 ifiers\, person-level and event-level records\, time dimensions\, geograph
 ic information\, update cycles and the implications of these structures fo
 r project design.</p><h4>Health and disability modules</h4><p>This session
  focuses on the main health and disability-related records available throu
 gh PLIDA\, including MBS\, PBS and NDIS data. It will cover module structu
 re\, file organisation\, coding systems\, coverage\, timing\, documentatio
 n and common interpretation issues specific to these records.</p><h4>Desig
 ning PLIDA analysis files</h4><p>This session puts the preceding material 
 into practice by working through examples of how research and policy quest
 ions can be translated into PLIDA analysis files. It will cover choices ab
 out study populations\, observation periods\, event records\, person-level
  summaries\, longitudinal structure\, and documentation of key decisions. 
 The session may include worked examples and project-based discussion to he
 lp participants think through how research questions can be implemented us
 ing PLIDA.</p><h4>Practical PLIDA workflows</h4><p>This session brings tog
 ether practical guidance for working efficiently and reliably in DataLab. 
 It will cover the PLIDA Modular Product Portal\, data dictionaries\, share
 d DataLab resources\, large-file management\, use of subsamples while deve
 loping code\, storage and memory constraints\, reproducible workflows\, qu
 ality checks\, output preparation\, clearance documentation and common mis
 takes to avoid.</p>\n</div>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</section>\n<!-- No ite
 ms found under root_nodes  -->\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</section>
 \n<section class="panel section3 Program-3">\n<h3 aria-controls="Program-3
